.TH LOGLAN-PARSE 1 "2026-10-15" "loglan-tools" "Loglan Tools"
.SH NAME
loglan-parse \- Randall Holmes's PEG parser for Loglan, with its built-in grammar and interactive loop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B loglan-parse
.RB [ \-i ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B loglan-parse
runs
.BR omnibus.py ,
Randall Holmes's parser for Loglan, built on Parsing Expression Grammars (PEG).
It runs the copy that
.B loglan-update
keeps current in
.BR ~/.loglan/src/Sources/omnibus.py ,
and it runs it in
.BR ~/.loglan/parse :
see
.BR FILES .
.PP
In
.BR omnibus.py ,
the PEG engine, the PEG rule compiler and the whole Loglan grammar
(about 500
.B L(\(dq...\(dq)
rule definitions) are all in this one file.
It needs no other modules.
.PP
It starts an interactive loop (see
.BR "INTERACTIVE COMMANDS" ).
Anything typed that is not a command is parsed as a Loglan utterance
with the default grammar class
.BR utterance .
The bracketed parse is printed and the input is appended to the log file.
.PP
With
.BR \-i ,
the interactive loop still starts first.
Leave it with
.B ...
to get the Python prompt, where you can call the module's functions directly (see
.BR FUNCTIONS ).
Call
.B interface()
to go back into the loop.
.SH OUTPUT
Each parse prints the input, a blank line, and then the parse tree.
In the tree,
.BI [ class : " ..." ]
marks a grammar class.
Any input that was not consumed comes after the tree.
Then the
.B Parser cache size
is printed, followed by a line of dashes.
If no parse is found, the output is
.BR fail .
.PP
The display settings at startup are:
.IP \(bu 2
indented output is on
.IP \(bu 2
shallow display is on, so a run of nested classes is shown as
.BI [ outer ": " inner : ...]
.IP \(bu 2
class names are shown
.IP \(bu 2
the
.B niceprecs()
settings are active.
These compact the little-word classes
.BR TAI0 ", " A1 ", " ACI ", " AGE ", " CA ", " ZE2 ", " I ", " ICA ", " ICI ", " IGE ", " KA ", " KI
into unlabelled strings, and mark
.B imperative
as important.
.SH INTERACTIVE COMMANDS
Each command is a single line.
In the commands below, the prefix character comes directly before its argument, with no space.
.TP
.B ...
Exit the loop.
.TP
.B ?
Show the command summary.
.TP
.BI ? line
Run
.B uttertest
on
.IR line .
This parses the longest initial segment that parses without failure.
It retries by removing one character at a time from the end, so it is very slow on long input.
.TP
.B ??
Turn off all compacted classes, for fully detailed parses.
Note: this line also begins with
.BR ? ,
so it additionally runs
.B uttertest
on the string
.RB \(dq ? \(dq.
.TP
.B !!
Restore the
.B niceprecs()
display settings and reset the default class to
.BR utterance .
.TP
.B |
Toggle the display of class names (verbose mode).
.TP
.BI : word
Parse
.I word
as a borrowing by parsing
.RB \(dq "liu " \fIword\fP\(dq
as class
.BR LIU ,
with compaction off for that parse.
.TP
.BI ^ class
Make
.I class
the default grammar class used to parse input.
.TP
.BI * rule
Show the definition of grammar rule
.IR rule .
.TP
.BI @ "name " "<- " expression
Add or replace a grammar rule for this session only.
The rule must pass the termination check (see
.BR "GRAMMAR SYNTAX" ).
.TP
.BI & line
Parse
.I line
without writing it to the log.
.TP
.BI # comment
Write
.I comment
to the log file.
.TP
.BI % name
Append the log from now on to
.IB name log.txt\fR.
.TP
.BI > name
Batch-parse
.IB name .txt
and write the results to
.IB name .llg\fR.
See
.BR "BATCH FILES" .
.TP
.BI < name
Turn an edited
.IB name .llg
back into
.IB name .txt
by removing the
.BR % -prefixed
parse lines.
.TP
.BI $ name
Export the current grammar as Python source to
.IB name .py\fR.
The exported file imports
.B loglanpreamble
and starts
.B interface()
when it is run.
.TP
.BI ` name
Export the current grammar, including comments, as PEG text to
.IB name .peg\fR.
.SH BATCH FILES
A batch input file
.IB name .txt
has one utterance per line.
.IP \(bu 2
A line starting with
.B #
is copied into the output as a comment.
.IP \(bu 2
A line starting with
.B %
is skipped.
.IP \(bu 2
A line ending in
.B \e
is joined to the line after it.
.PP
In the output
.IB name .llg\fR,
each utterance is followed by its parse.
Every parse line starts with
.BR % ,
so
.B <
can remove them again.
.SH GRAMMAR SYNTAX
Rules use Bryan Ford's PEG notation, without ASCII code escapes:
.PP
.RS
.nf
name <- expression
e1 e2        sequence
e1 / e2      ordered choice
e?  e*  e+   optional, zero or more, one or more
&e  !e       and-predicate, not-predicate
( e )        grouping
\(dqabc\(dq 'abc'  literal string
[a-zA-Z]     character class
\&.            any character
.fi
.RE
.PP
Escape sequences
.BR \en ", " \et ", " \er ", " \e' ", " \e\(dq ", " \e[ ", " \e] " and " \e\e
can be used in literals and character classes.
A definition line that starts with
.B #
or
.B <
is stored as a comment.
.PP
Each new rule is checked for termination.
A rule that might match without consuming input in a way that could loop is rejected with
.BR "Termination check failure in" " \fIname\fP" .
Rule names that start with
.B _
skip this check.
.SH FUNCTIONS
These functions are useful after
.BR "loglan-parse \-i" :
.TP
.BI utter( s ") / Utter(" s )
Parse
.I s
as the default class.
.B utter
returns the parse as a string; the capitalized version prints it.
.TP
.BI utteras( class ", " s ") / Utteras(" class ", " s )
Parse
.I s
as a specific grammar class.
.TP
.BI uttertest( s ") / Uttertest(" s )
Find the longest initial segment of
.I s
that parses.
.TP
.BI L( rule )
Define a grammar rule, the same as
.BR @ .
.TP
.BI Showrule( name )
Print a rule.
.TP
.BI filebatch( name ") / revbatch(" name )
The same as the
.B >
and
.B <
commands.
.TP
.BI saverules( name ") / saverules2(" name )
The same as the
.B $
and
.B `
commands.
.TP
.BI grammarbatch( name )
Discard the built-in grammar and load all rules from
.IB name .peg\fR.
.TP
.B Verbose() Shallow() Indent() Numberbrackets() Greedy()
Toggle, in order: class names; shallow display; indented display;
.BI < n >
depth markers on brackets; greedy parsing.
Greedy parsing always takes the longest successful alternative, and it is slow.
If greedy and normal parsing give different results, the grammar has a formal problem.
.TP
.BI Compact( class ") / Decompact()"
Show a class as a plain unlabelled string, or clear all such settings.
.TP
.BI MakeImportant( class ") / DevalueImportance()"
Mark a class as important, so that decluttering does not hide it, or clear all such marks.
.TP
.B niceprecs()
Apply the default compaction settings.
.TP
.B interface2()
An alternative interactive loop.
It prints parses with
.B %
in front of every line, in the
.B .llg
style.
.SH FILES
.B loglan-parse
runs the parser in
.B ~/.loglan/parse
(or
.B $LOGLAN_STATE_DIR/parse\fR),
since the parser reads and writes its files in the directory it runs in.
A name given to the
.BR > ,
.BR < ,
.BR % ,
.B $
or
.B `
command is therefore taken from there, unless it is a full path:
.B >/home/me/text
batch-parses
.BR /home/me/text.txt .
.PP
At startup, the parser creates or truncates
.B dummy
and
.B dummy2
there.
It also opens
.B dummylog
for appending, and this is the log file until
.B %
selects another.
.TP
.IB name log.txt
Log file chosen with
.BR % .
.TP
.IB name .txt ", " name .llg
Batch input and output files.
.TP
.IB name .py ", " name .peg
Exported grammars.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LOGLAN_STATE_DIR
The data directory, instead of
.BR ~/.loglan .
.SH NOTES
Parse results are cached for each rule and input position.
The cache is cleared before every top-level parse.
.PP
The source is published at
.UR https://randall-holmes.github.io/Loglan/Sources/omnibus.py
.UE ;
loglan-tools ships a copy, and
.B loglan-update
replaces it when the published one changes.
